    <h1 class="fra-hero-h1">
      Full Retirement Age Calculator: Find Your Social Security FRA
    </h1>
    <p class="fra-hero-date">Figures last verified against official sources in July 2026.</p>
    
    <div class="fra-aeo-block" style="background: #0A3D3A; border-radius: 0.75rem; padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem; margin: 1.25rem 0;">
      <p style="font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: 'uppercase'; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: #C9933A; margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;">Quick Answer</p>
      <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #ffffff; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">Your Social Security Full Retirement Age is 67 if you were born in 1960 or later. Claiming at 62 permanently reduces your benefit by up to 30%. Waiting until 70 increases it by 24% above your FRA amount via delayed retirement credits of 8% per year.</p>
    </div>

    <ul class="fra-who-for" style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 1rem 0 1rem 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem;">
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-text-muted, #4B5A6E);"><span aria-hidden="true" style="color: #0A3D3A; font-weight: 700; flex-shrink: 0;">→</span> Adults approaching 62 who want to know their exact Full Retirement Age before deciding when to file for Social Security.</li>
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-text-muted, #4B5A6E);"><span aria-hidden="true" style="color: #0A3D3A; font-weight: 700; flex-shrink: 0;">→</span> Workers born between 1943–1959 whose FRA falls between 66 and 67 years old and want the exact month-level calculation.</li>
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-text-muted, #4B5A6E);"><span aria-hidden="true" style="color: #0A3D3A; font-weight: 700; flex-shrink: 0;">→</span> Spouses or adult children helping a family member understand the reduction percentage for filing before FRA.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="fra-hero-sub">
      Enter your exact date of birth to find your official Social Security Full Retirement Age, exact calendar date, and benefit percentage at 62, FRA, and 70. Free — no signup, no account, no email required.
    </p>
    <p class="fra-hero-source">
      Based on official Social Security Administration (SSA) benefit guidelines &amp; 1st-of-the-month rules. Last reviewed: {lastReviewed}. Not financial advice.
    </p>
  </div>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       2. INTERACTIVE TOOL (FRONT AND CENTER)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="fra-tool-block" id="calculator">
    <FRACalculatorDynamic client:load />
  </div>

  <NoSignupStatement />


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       3. PLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="fra-section" aria-labelledby="explanation-heading">
    <h2 id="explanation-heading">What Your Full Retirement Age Means — and Why It Matters</h2>

    <p>
      Your <strong>Full Retirement Age (FRA)</strong> — also known as your Normal Retirement Age by the Social Security Administration — is the exact age at which you are entitled to receive 100% of your unreduced monthly Social Security benefit (your Primary Insurance Amount, or PIA).
    </p>

    <h3>Why Searching Birth Years One by One Leaves Confusion</h3>
    <p>
      Many retirees search static tables for their birth year (such as "born in 1958" or "born in 1959") and end up unsure about the exact calendar date their full benefits start. Entering your exact birthdate resolves three common traps:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Exact Months Count:</strong> Birth years between 1955 and 1959 add 2 months per year (e.g. 1957 = 66 years and 6 months). Our calculator pinpoints the exact month and year your check reaches 100%.</li>
      <li><strong>The SSA 1st-of-Month Birthday Rule:</strong> If you were born on the 1st day of any month, Social Security legally treats you as being born in the preceding month. This moves your FRA one month earlier.</li>
      <li><strong>Earnings Test Freedom:</strong> Reaching your exact FRA date ends the Social Security earnings penalty, allowing you to work and earn unlimited income without benefit withholding.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="fra-info-box">
      <p class="fra-info-box-heading">The Financial Difference Between Claiming at 62 vs. FRA vs. 70</p>
      <p>
        If your Full Retirement Age is 67, claiming early at age 62 permanently locks in a <strong>30% monthly check reduction</strong>. Conversely, delaying past FRA earns 8% per year in Delayed Retirement Credits, resulting in a <strong>24% permanent bonus at age 70</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       4. WORKED EXAMPLE (STEP BY STEP)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="fra-section fra-section--tinted" aria-labelledby="worked-example-heading">
    <h2 id="worked-example-heading">Step-by-Step Worked Example: Henry Born on March 15, 1958</h2>
    <p>
      Here is how the <strong>full retirement age calculator</strong> computes Henry's exact FRA and benefit percentages.
    </p>

    <div class="fra-example-card">
      <div class="fra-example-scenario">
        <span class="fra-example-label">Scenario</span>
        <p>
          <strong>Henry</strong> was born on <strong>March 15, 1958</strong>. His estimated baseline monthly benefit (PIA) at Full Retirement Age is <strong>$2,200</strong> per month.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="fra-example-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Determine FRA Years &amp; Months:</strong>
          For birth year 1958, SSA rules set FRA at <strong>66 years and 8 months</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Calculate Exact FRA Calendar Date:</strong>
          Adding 66 years and 8 months to March 15, 1958 gives <strong>November 15, 2024</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Claiming at Age 62 (March 2020):</strong>
          Henry receives <strong>71.7% of PIA</strong> = $1,577.40/month (a permanent 28.3% reduction).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Claiming at FRA (November 2024):</strong>
          Henry receives <strong>100% of PIA</strong> = $2,200/month.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Claiming at Age 70 (March 2028):</strong>
          Henry receives <strong>126.7% of PIA</strong> = $2,787.40/month (a permanent $587.40/month bonus).
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div style="background: #fff8f0; border-left: 4px solid #E8761A; border-radius: 0 0.5rem 0.5rem 0; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 700; color: #E8761A; margin: 0 0 0.25rem 0;">💡 Pro Tip</p>
        <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">Your exact FRA matters for spousal and survivor benefit calculations too. If you are coordinating benefits with a spouse, enter both birthdates separately and compare your FRA months — a difference of even 2–3 months can shift your optimal filing strategy.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="fra-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Confusing Your 65th Birthday with Full Retirement Age</h3>
    <p>Many people assume they can retire at 65 with full benefits because that was the historical norm and remains the age for Medicare. For anyone born in 1960 or later, FRA is 67. Claiming at 65 will result in a permanent reduction.</p>
    <p>The fix is <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/retirement/planner/1943.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: #0A3D3A; text-decoration: underline;">checking the official SSA FRA chart by birth year</a>.</p>

    <h3>Thinking Delayed Credits Continue After Age 70</h3>
    <p>While delaying benefits past your FRA earns you an 8% annual bonus, these delayed retirement credits permanently stop accumulating the month you turn 70. Waiting to claim after 70 just leaves money on the table without any further benefit increase.</p>
    <p>The fix is claiming your benefits at age 70 at the latest.</p>

    <h3>Forgetting the First-of-the-Month Born Rule</h3>
    <p>Social Security has a quirky legal rule: if you are born on the first day of any month, they calculate your benefits as if you were born the month prior. If you don't account for this, your expected full retirement date will be off by a month.</p>
    <p>The fix is using your exact birthdate in this calculator, which automatically handles the 1st-of-the-month rule.</p>
  </section>

